  "species_found": "Diffuse knapweed (Centaurea diffusa)",
  "lifecycle_info": "Diffuse knapweed is a biennial to short-lived perennial weed. In its first year (or early growth phase), it forms a low-growing rosette of finely divided leaves close to the ground. Bolting and flowering typically occur in late spring to summer of the second year. Rosettes can be present in early spring or in the fall after germination from late-season seeds.",
  "control_methods": "Chemical and mechanical controls are most effective at the rosette stage. The correct timing window for treatment is early spring (when plants are actively growing but before bolting) or in the fall (after germination and before winter dormancy). Herbicide applications during these periods are highly effective because plants are small and actively translocating nutrients to roots. Mechanical control such as hand-pulling or digging is also effective at the rosette stage if the taproot is fully removed. Treatments are less effective once the plant bolts or flowers, so applications should be completed before stem elongation."
